Janis Papanagnou wrote:

> Benjamin Lewis wrote:
>> David Damerell wrote:
>>
>>> I'd make WoW unrechargeable and un-last-charge-squeezable, and give the
>>> Castle wand a guaranteed 3 charges.
>> Why not 2 charges, unrechargeable, and keep it squeezable?  I kind of
>> like that the very last wish is not a reliable emergency measure.
>> Another possibility is giving a guarantee of two, and a possibility of a
>> third, so that the castle wand usually gives 3 total wishes, but
>> occasionally 4.  Or you could do something like 2:15%, 3:70%, 4:15%.  I
>> definitely agree that 7 is too many.  I rarely, at this point, get as
>> far as the castle wand, but if I do I'm almost guaranteed to ascend.
>
> Assuming you identify the /WoW immediately (otherwise the use of the wand
> is delayed)...

I was talking about the castle wand in this case.

> If you intend to immediately use wishes in the early game to have a
> benefit right away then I think you have to count differently.  The wand
> may have 1..3 charges (plus rechargability).  In early game you need to
> first wish for scrolls of recharging to obtain your goal of immediate
> utilization.  Makes 0..2 charges left.  Then you have to identify the
> wand as to not risk wresting out the last wish (been there); at that
> point you may or may not have identify. If not then you should wait and
> will not have an immediate gain from the wand (or play russian roulette
> and zap it).  If you have identify it's easier and have 3..5(+1) charges
> for arbitrary items (not 7).

I'd still count the recharging scrolls as a wish, since you generally will
get two of them.  Not as powerful as some of the items typically wished for
certainly, but still ... maybe count it as half a wish or something.  If
you don't find any other charging scrolls and it's an early game wand it
can effectively get you another wish from the castle wand too.

> Given these restrictions in early game *and* all that if/then/else *and*
> the remaining variance *and* the low probability of the generation of a
> /WoW then I question the importance of what we are talking about here.
>
> That's for the "early" /WoW.

My inclination would be to just make the wand unrechargeable.  That reduces
it to 1..3(+1) charges period, which I think is a significant difference,
both for early game and for the castle wand.  The more wishes you get the
less likely it is that you'll have to make tough decisions about what to
wish for.  These tough decisions make the game more interesting, in my
opinion.

> Finding one is a bright spot in a set of more or less lucky games, as the
> wand of death in a gnomes hand is the opposite thereof.

2..4 wishes is still a really bright spot, though.  I also consider finding
a magic lamp, or getting a wish from a throne, potion, etc. to be a bright
spot in a game, and you can't even save the throne wishes for later.

I like the magic lamp gamble -- either an unlimited light source, or a
*possibility* of a wish, leading to a strong encouragement not to use the
wish unless it's *really* necessary.

